# Color Styling Rules

## Primary Rule: Always Use Colors from shared/constants/theme.css

**NEVER use hardcoded colors, hex values, or direct CSS color values in components.** Always use the colors defined in `shared/constants/theme.css` via Tailwind utility classes.

## Usage Pattern

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
// Use direct Tailwind classes from theme.css
<div className="bg-background text-foreground">
  <p className="text-foreground-secondary">Secondary text</p>
  <button className="bg-brand text-foreground">Brand button</button>
  <div className="border-destructive bg-destructive/10 text-destructive">
    Error message
  </div>
  <div className="text-success bg-success/20 border-success">
    Success message
  </div>
  <div className="text-warning bg-warning/20">Warning message</div>
</div>
```

❌ **INCORRECT:**

```tsx
// Don't use hardcoded colors
<div className="bg-white text-black">
<div style={{ color: '#000000' }}>
<div className="text-[#000000]">

// Don't use colors that aren't defined in shared/constants/theme.css
// Check theme.css to see what colors are available
```

## Opacity/Transparency

You can use Tailwind opacity modifiers with these colors:

```tsx
<div className="bg-brand/20 border-brand/60 text-foreground/80">
<div className="bg-success/10 text-success/90">
<div className="border-destructive/50 bg-destructive/5">
```

## Theme Awareness

All colors in `shared/constants/theme.css` are theme-aware and automatically support light/dark mode. The CSS variables are defined for both `:root` (light mode) and `.dark` (dark mode), so Tailwind classes automatically adapt to the current theme.

## When to Use This Rule

- When adding new components or styling existing ones
- When choosing colors for text, backgrounds, borders, or any visual elements
- When creating new UI components or modifying existing ones
- When working with Tailwind classes that involve colors

## Notes

- Colors are defined in `shared/constants/theme.css` as CSS variables
- The `@theme` directive in `theme.css` maps these CSS variables to Tailwind utilities (e.g., `--color-success: var(--success)` enables `text-success`, `bg-success`, etc.)
- `theme.css` is imported in `website/app/globals.css`
- Always check `shared/constants/theme.css` to see what colors are available - the color list may change over time
- Only use colors that exist in `theme.css`
- If you need a new color, add it to `theme.css` first (both `:root` and `.dark` selectors), then add it to the `@theme` block, then use it via Tailwind classes
